  "prediction": "**Early Withdrawals of Retirement Savings: Incidence, Amount, and Strategies to Reduce**\n\nThe Government Accountability Office (GAO) has released a report examining the incidence and amount of early withdrawals from retirement savings, including Individual Retirement Accounts (IRAs) and 401(k) plans. The report highlights the significant impact of early withdrawals on retirement savings and identifies strategies to reduce this behavior.\n\n**Incidence and Amount of Early Withdrawals**\n\nThe report estimates that in 2013, individuals ages 25 to 55 withdrew approximately $68.7 billion from their retirement accounts, with IRA owners accounting for 57% of these withdrawals. The report also found that the incidence of early withdrawals was higher among individuals with certain demographic and economic characteristics, such as lower income, education, and household debt.\n\n**Factors Leading to Early Withdrawals**\n\nThe report identifies several factors that contribute to early withdrawals, including:\n\n*   Financial stress and limited financial literacy\n*   Job loss or separation\n*   Medical expenses and other unexpected financial needs\n*   Limited access to emergency savings\n\n**Strategies to Reduce Early Withdrawals**\n\nThe report suggests several strategies to reduce early withdrawals, including:\n\n*   Simplifying the rollover process for 401(k) plans\n*   Restricting cashouts to participant contributions only\n*   Narrowing the IRS safe harbor for hardship withdrawals\n*   Replacing hardship withdrawals with hardship loans\n*   Incorporating emergency savings features into 401(k) plans\n\n**Recommendations**\n\nThe report recommends that the Secretary of Labor direct the Assistant Secretary for EBSA to revise the Form 5500 to require plan sponsors to report qualified plan loan offsets as a separate line item distinct from other types of distributions.\n\n**Conclusion**\n\nThe report highlights the importance of addressing early withdrawals of retirement savings to ensure the long-term security of retirement plans. By implementing strategies to reduce early withdrawals, employers and policymakers can help individuals build a more secure retirement.",
